<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  App-level privacy manifest.

  This file is the machine-readable form of the promise the ดวง screen makes
  in Thai: birth data stays on the device. The app collects nothing, tracks
  nothing, and calls no tracking domains.

  What never leaves the phone: the birth date, the dream journal, saved ticket
  numbers, the monthly budget. There is no account to attach them to and no
  code that uploads them.

  What does leave it, and must be stated plainly: the dream text typed on the
  ความฝัน screen is sent to the content library to be matched against the ตำรา,
  because the matching is done in SQL on the server. The request carries no
  identifier — no account, no device id, no advertising id — and the server
  keeps nothing: the miss log records the LENGTH of an unmatched dream and
  never its text (migration 20260729001000, asserted by the SQL suite). Under
  Apple's definition that is servicing a request in real time, not collection,
  which is why NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes below is empty. If the server ever
  starts retaining that text, this file and the App Store privacy label are
  both wrong the same day.

  UserDefaults (via shared_preferences) is accessed for the user's OWN local
  data — journal, saved tickets, budget, birth date. CA92.1 is the declared
  reason: access from the app itself, to store app state. The plugin ships
  its own manifest; this one covers the app target.
-->
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
